# SSH Server Configuration

## 1. Purpose
This file documents the SSH settings relevant to secure server access.
The main SSH server settings are defined in `/etc/ssh/sshd_config`.

## 2. SSH Port
The default SSH port `22` is changed to `17222`:
    Port 17222
Example connection:
    ssh -p 17222 admin@<SERVER_IP>

## 3. Authentication and Access Control

The server uses public key authentication. Password and interactive authentication are disabled.
    PermitRootLogin no
    PubkeyAuthentication yes
    PasswordAuthentication no
    KbdInteractiveAuthentication no
    AllowUsers admin deploy

- `PermitRootLogin no` — disables direct root login.
- `PubkeyAuthentication yes` — enables public key authentication.
- `PasswordAuthentication no` — disables password authentication.
- `KbdInteractiveAuthentication no` — disables keyboard-interactive authentication.
- `AllowUsers admin deploy` — allows SSH access only for the specified users.

## 4. Configuration Verification
Check the SSH configuration syntax:
    sudo sshd -t

Display the effective SSH configuration parameters:
    sudo sshd -T